UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 9, Lesson 1: Colonies across Australia', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"British colonies spread across Australia through the 1800s (for example New South Wales, Van Diemen’s Land/Tasmania, Western Australia, South Australia, Victoria, Queensland) on different dates. Expansion meant more pressure on First Nations Countries.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy does a colony foundation date not mean the land was empty before that year?\n\nFEEDBACK\nFirst Nations Peoples already lived there; the date marks British colonial government, not the start of human history.\n\nWATCH FOR\nLearning colony names without First Nations presence repeats erasure.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nBuild a colonies timeline with six foundation dates from a published map/text. Add a caption about First Nations Countries. Do not invent Nation names for each colony.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"British colonies spread across Australia through the 1800s (for example New South Wales, Van Diemen’s Land/Tasmania, Western Australia, South Australia, Victoria, Queensland) on different dates. Expansion meant more pressure on First Nations Countries.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Build a colonies timeline with six foundation dates from a published map/text. Add a caption about First Nations Countries. Do not invent Nation names for each colony. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: First Nations Peoples already lived there; the date marks British colonial government, not the start of human history. Reasoning: I used colony by attending to Australia rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Learning colony names without First Nations presence repeats erasure. Correction: First Nations Peoples already lived there; the date marks British colonial government, not the start of human history. Why: the correction restores accurate use of colony.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Learning colony names without First Nations presence repeats erasure. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating colony on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with colony, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"First Nations Peoples already lived there; the date marks British colonial government, not the start of human history. This matches the lesson skill for colony.","options":["First Nations Peoples already lived there; the date marks British colonial government, not the start of human history.","Learn